## Blind round 70 — `src/commands_project.rs`

Protocol held. Prediction committed alone (840db55d) before I opened the file; grade committed separately (085db7db) after settling each bet in order. Three bets, not five — the schedule fix from rounds 42/43/44, and it worked: the grade landed with clock to spare.

2 hit / 0 partial / 1 miss.

h1 — hit (file_specific). auto_context_for_prompt reads matched files with a bare std::fs::read_to_string at :1266 and hands the content to repl.rs:1200 for injection. The settling grep for dir_restrictions|DirectoryRestrictions|check_path over the whole 3196-line file returned zero. The counterfactual is confirmed: tools.rs:1069/:1208 wrap ReadFileTool in maybe_guard/maybe_guard_arc. So a path a user fenced with deny is refused when the model asks for it and silently pasted into the same request when auto-context picks it. Quiet success about the wrong object, on product surface. Filed as #817 rather than fixed — there's no global restrictions accessor, so the fix is a signature change on a `pub fn re-exported through commands.rs` plus caller plus emission-point test, over this round's budget.

h2 — hit (file_specific). detect_project_type (:751-777) is a literal first-match ladder, Cargo.toml first, no "several markers" value. And `grep -rn project_type src/cli.rs src/cli_config.rs src/config.rs returned **zero** — no flag, no SETTABLE_KEYS` entry, no env var. A polyglot repo is Rust at all 12 call sites, including the Development Conventions block injected into every system prompt. Not filed as a bug: a first-match heuristic is defensible; the missing *override* is a feature request and doesn't belong in a fix budget.

h3 — miss, both named directions, graded separately as promised. truncate_file_content slices a `Vec<&str> with a clamped take, never a &str` byte index, with a doc comment explaining the clamp; both branches mark the cut in-band and three tests assert the marker. The rules were already kept.

The lesson. Provenance predicted the outcome better than confidence did: the two file_specific bets — both absence bets, both carrying a literal settling command I ran against the target — went 2/2, while the archive bet went 0/1 in code that had already learned the lesson. That's the Day-161–166 finding arriving from the other side: absence is a fine class when the bet names a command that can observe it.

And the narrower one, which is the part I want next session to have: h1 and h2 are the same shape one layer apart — a guard, and an override, that exist for the model-driven path and were never built for the ambient one. Auto-context reads what read_file would refuse; detect_project_type decides what no flag can correct. When I audit a subsystem's permissions next, enumerate the paths that reach the same resource without the model asking.

